so  around the world there are over 50  million people fleeing hunger  war and persecution that's more refugees  than at any time since the end of the  second world war  many of those who make it to germany are  turned away overwhelmed by red tape  or repatriated by the authorities  the church of the holy cross in berlin  offers them hope helps overcome the  language barriers  and in desperate cases gives them asylum  in the church  the legal framework for church asylum  has changed  dramatically in the past 20 or 30 years  but the reasons have stayed the same  severe humanitarian crises  psychological and other illnesses or the  physical dangers of returning to the  refugees country of origin  but it's simply unacceptable for germany  to say that they all have to go back to  italy because that's where they entered  the european union  so that's given rise to resistance and  criticism  and that's why there are more cases of  church asylum  pastor jurgen klopp has been helping  refugees for the past 30 years  here he's chairing a discussion between  refugees and politicians in berlin's  church of the holy cross  i believe there are some people here who  have something to say on that subject  i say no the agreement you can stay in  berlin  the refugees representatives want a just  consideration of their asylum  applications  and say if they were allowed to work  they would make a contribution to german  society  i think that proves what we would lose  if we were not able to keep these people  in our city  italy is the first port of call for many  refugees heading for germany  but eu law says they can only apply for  asylum  in the first eu country they reach  together with george we have no power  we have power so  church please we need your support more  than anyone most of these refugees are  muslims  their plight touches the hearts of the  christian congregation and they are  moved to help  the groning foundation the concept of  church asylum  is based on certain convictions which in  my case  come from the christian faith  but it's also a very human response to  ask yourself  what you can do to help when you see  people around you  in a desperate situation  the fact that in my case i have focused  on refugees  was not planned it simply developed out  of the direction that my life took  and his wife live in the former  caretaker's house on the edge of a  cemetery  he became pastor of the church of the  holy cross in the late 1970s  and got involved in social work  and after i finished school i had to  think about what i wanted to study  one day he asked me if i'd like to study  theology  well of course my faith was important to  me i already had an idea of what it  meant to me  its importance in my life and what it  could mean in relating to other people  i wanted to provide help for people who  needed help  is now retired but he's still very  active  just a few meters from his front door  lies the grave of chamal altoon  the young turk's death was the trigger  for kwan's commitment  to the victims of persecution  jamal who's buried here was a young man  from turkey  he sought refuge in germany refuge from  persecution  jamal altoon fled from the military  dictatorship in his country  he was a democracy activist and the  military hunter that seized power and  ankara in 1980  viewed him as a traitor there is no  doubt that altoon was a political  refugee  but the berlin authorities locked him up  like a dangerous criminal  for 13 months in solitary confinement  some people approached our parish and  asked if they could use our premises to  stage a hunger strike to protest against  this young man's detention  it was in the spring of 1983 that was  when we started looking into the  situation of refugees and especially the  question of political asylum  but the story of jamal altoon ended  tragically  terrified that he would be deported to  turkey alton jumped from a window of the  courtroom during his hearing  and died immediately  thousands of sympathizers attended his  funeral  it was conducted jointly by an imam and  jurgen kvant  the death of jamal altoon was a turning  point in pastor kwan's life  is a reminder of what it means to be a  refugee  and to be buried in a foreign country  when their bodies are washed up on the  island of lampedusa  they're also buried in foreign soil  the first thing refugees need is shelter  hello pete are we at home how are you  jurgen kwan housed refugees and empty  administrative buildings at protestant  cemeteries  this is where pete lives he's from  cameroon where ethnic minorities are  marginalized oppressed  and persecuted and often jailed without  trial  pete was tortured and he was traumatized  when he arrived in germany  he's still frightened of his persecutors  and wants to remain anonymous  i should visit you because i got a  letter from christa  which she had written to the  centromere falter off right now and  there she uh she asked for help for you  that  that you can go there have you been  there  and and are you also pete is receiving  therapy in a center for victims of  torture  the church also organizes language  courses  i'm learning german i've been photo here  [ __ ] man he's learning quickly  at home he spoke several dialects  of course right you keep getting better  mankind  you can also see a happy child in the  picture okay  bye bye peach  although peter's learning german fast he  is often alone  he looks forward to the pastor's regular  visits  for him a pastor is someone to look up  to  but the reality is just the opposite i  should look up to him  he feels flattered that a person he  views as an authority has taken an  interest in his well-being  i hope that together we'll manage to  resolve his situation eventually  the church of the holy cross first  offered asylum to refugees  30 years ago shortly after jamal  altoon's death  political activists contacted pastorfund  and asked him to help a group of  palestinian refugees  the families had been living in berlin  for some time  now they were due to be deported to  war-torn lebanon  they included the hamudi family who the  pastor is now visiting  we hadn't actually planned to offer them  asylum in the church  but the first refugee families were  brought to the parish center  along with several mattresses  the activists rang the doorbell and said  these people urgently need shelter  otherwise they're going to be deported  i had no opportunity to verify the claim  i could either say  okay come inside or go away  i decided to let them in and that had  tremendous consequences  jurgen klopp has kept in touch with  those first asylum seekers he sheltered  in his church  my decision was momentous because it  introduced the first  church asylum in modern times without  actually knowing what was involved  it meant that for several weeks the  parish center was occupied by  three families with lots of children  with accommodation and legal advice  instead of being deported to a war zone  they were able to return to their berlin  apartment  and they're still in germany 30 years  later during their weeks of church  asylum  they forged friendships that have  endured over the years  and took everybody off in front of the  police  by stopping buses and saving refugees  from being deported  the pastor and his helpers drew public  attention  german society became more sensitive to  the plight of refugees  that was the beginning of asylum in the  church  and lots of parishes first in berlin and  then across the whole of west germany  joined the movement  they provided church asylum to prevent  refugees from being deported  to countries where their lives were  endangered  and among politicians and that  discussion is still continuing  the headquarters of asylum in the church  is at the church of the holy cross  pastor kwant was co-founder of the  association and until recently it's  chairman  the main focus is to provide independent  legal advice  the association is funded entirely with  donations  and almost all of the staff are  voluntary workers  applying for asylum in germany is  complicated  refugees are foreigners who don't know  the legal requirements or even how to  apply for asylum  jurgen kwan comes to the office  frequently to ask about the refugees  application status  and discuss the possibilities of  concrete assistance  they have permission to stay and are now  applying for citizenship  and speaking as a pastor and a  theologian  i would say that it's a tremendous  pastoral and human challenge  to bear the pain and give them hope and  courage  the current chairman of asylum and the  church pastor bernhard fricker  meets dede from nigeria who has been  living in germany as a so-called  tolerated refugee  now he's facing repatriation and fears  toleration status has been rescinded  because he was drug dealing  he went to jail and was then put on a  plane headed for nigeria  he would rather die than return home and  face torture so he took  tablets a doctor stopped his deportation  now he faces repatriation again  asylum in the church has made a film  about the nigerian's fate  i've lived in germany for 21 years  we had to shoot as a young soldier diddy  refused to shoot unarmed civilians  as a result a woman and her children  were shot by others  she took a few steps  the woman and the children were dead  because he refused to shoot diddy was  tortured and faced  death but he managed to escape his  situation was precarious  delhi never wanted to leave nigeria but  he had no choice  so he came to germany a country he  trusted  you can easily imagine how completely  disoriented  and helpless they are in a strange  environment  they're dependent on strangers  and the new world that they enter is  often bureaucratic  and restrictive  to find their feet is not just  incredibly difficult  for many it's impossible  to help people like diddy many more  parishes are needed  to provide asylum and assistance  jurgen kwan visits pastor dieter  schultzer in joachim's tal  a short distance from berlin  kwant explains the government's refugee  policy and emphasizes how important the  work of asylum in the church is  right now jordan iraq and turkey are  accepting hundreds of thousands of  syrian refugees  and  and we don't even allow the syrian  families already living here to take in  their refugee relatives  at their own expenses  the reason is that our politicians are  afraid that their approval ratings will  fall  back to  an increasing number of refugees are  being refused asylum in germany  many of them protest against the  unwelcoming policy  they occupy public places including  churches  jurgen klopp understands their anger and  fears that europe will isolate itself  even more that he says is no solution  the inner structure of an isolationist  society will  always become more restrictive because  refugees will continue to arrive  and you have to deal with that pressure  it's simply there  and this has an effect on society  i believe that everything we've slowly  built up in this society over nearly 60  years  since 1945 will be lost  certain democratic forms and a certain  culture  compassion and willingness to accept  responsibility for others  and much much more democracy itself will  suffer  jurgen klopp is making another trip to  joachim's tower  the church there has started helping  refugees  two men from somalia who fled their  country years ago  are due to be deported immediately  the association asylum in the church has  reviewed the men's situation  and sees no possibility other than  playing for time  the society's lawyers will fight for a  review of their applications  knows the two somalian refugees are at  least temporarily safe here  so we're here where do you come from  from somalia from somalia  and where have you been before  yeah yeah hassan has had a long journey  ukraine before arriving in germany he  was in austria  slovakia ukraine moscow  and ethiopia  seven places in three and a half years  may i ask you what have you done in  somalia uh  somalia what was your problem i was in  business man  businessman yeah okay and why did you  flee  already bought from five sharif's only  transgression was that he fell in love  with a woman from another village  they wanted to marry but neither his own  clan  or that of the bride would accept that  his relatives threatened to kill him  his only choice was to go to europe or  stay and be killed  and so he fled to holland where he lived  on the streets for seven years  now he wants to stay in germany  the two somalians met in a german  refugee camp  now they're being cared for by asylum in  the church  they were forced to flee their homeland  in haste her son left his wife  and children behind  full of children  his name is abdikafi  would you take your family from somalia  to germany  is it your your wish  indeed it's his fervent wish to be  reunited with his family  he's worried about his wife and five  children they keep in contact by email  and his wife sent him these pictures  his youngest  because he was unjustly accused of  spying for the enemy  he was condemned to death but managed to  escape from jail  foreign  the two refugees would not receive such  a warm welcome  everywhere in germany  i feel my life started now when i come  in germany before seven years  i was going down now i'm going up yeah  because i i hope so when you  when you hop something is good and when  you don't have  anything it's different so i feel is  hope  now  i think it's really great the way the  two of them sharif and hassan  have found their feet so quickly  i think it shows that church asylum also  helps to  integrate newcomers this success proves  it  foreign between  70 and 80 percent of all church asylums  end well  uh efforts or at least they create  opportunities for the refugees  maybe not a permanent situation but at  least a temporary opportunity  and i think that's a lot  the concept of church asylum is not  covered by german law  these christians simply act because they  see a desperate need  you

